## A letter to a foundation

In the summer of 1955 John McCarthy, a young mathematician at Dartmouth College in New Hampshire, decided to bring together the people who were thinking about thinking machines. There weren’t many of them, and they were scattered: some at universities, some in industrial labs. To pay for a meeting, he needed to ask a foundation.

McCarthy brought in three co-authors. Marvin Minsky was then at Harvard, working on neural networks. Nathaniel Rochester led the design of one of IBM’s first production computers. Claude Shannon of Bell Labs was already famous as the founder of information theory. Shannon’s and Rochester’s names gave the proposal a weight that the young McCarthy and Minsky didn’t yet have.

The proposal is dated 31 August 1955. It opens like a business plan: a study of artificial intelligence by ten people over two months in the summer of 1956, at Dartmouth College.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: who signed the proposal, and where did each of them work?</summary>

McCarthy (Dartmouth College), Minsky (Harvard), Rochester (IBM) and Shannon (Bell Labs).

</details>

## A name for a new field

The proposal’s title used words science hadn’t had before: “artificial intelligence.” McCarthy chose them. He later explained that one reason was to escape the word “cybernetics,” which would have meant either accepting its coiner, Norbert Wiener, as a guru or arguing with him.

The name turned out to be both a gift and a trap. A gift, because it gave people in different labs one banner to gather under. A trap, because “intelligence” promises more than any program of the day could deliver. That gap between the name and the ability has followed the field through its whole history, right up to today’s arguments.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why didn’t McCarthy call the field cybernetics?</summary>

By his own account, to keep it clear of Norbert Wiener: neither taking him as an authority nor having to argue with him.

</details>

## A bold assumption

The heart of the proposal is a single sentence. The study was to proceed on the basis of the conjecture that “every aspect of learning or any other feature of intelligence can in principle be so precisely described that a machine can be made to simulate it.”

That’s a bet, not a fact, and the authors honestly called it a conjecture. A research program follows from it: if everything can be described, describe it. The proposal lists seven problems:
- automatic computers, and how to write programs for them;
- how to program a computer to use a language;
- neuron nets;
- a theory of how much computation a problem takes;
- self-improvement;
- abstractions;
- randomness and creativity.

Nearly all of them are still research topics today, seventy years on.

Then the main promise. The authors believed that a significant advance could be made on one or more of these problems if a carefully selected group of scientists worked on them together for a summer. Notice how careful it is: not “solve” but “advance,” and not on every problem but on at least one.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what was the proposal’s central assumption?</summary>

That every aspect of learning and intelligence can be described so precisely that a machine can simulate it.

</details>

## What happened that summer

They asked the Rockefeller Foundation for $13,500 and got $7,500, for five weeks. The workshop ran in the summer of 1956: from about 18 June to 17 August, roughly eight weeks, according to the notes of one participant, Ray Solomonoff; Nils Nilsson, an AI researcher, gives six weeks in his history of the field. Disagreement like this is normal here: nobody kept minutes.

Ten people for two months never quite happened. People came for a few days or weeks and left. The list of participants was pieced together later from letters and notes, and it varies between sources: twenty names in one, more than thirty in another. They agree on one thing: only three, Solomonoff, Minsky and McCarthy, were there all summer. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon visited briefly, in the first weeks, and unlike most of the others they brought a program that had already proved theorems on paper, by hand simulation.

No breakthrough came that summer. Something else did: the field got a name, a group of people who began calling themselves its researchers, and a list of problems. In the following years, workshop participants founded the first AI labs. That’s why Dartmouth is called the field’s birth, even though nothing was invented there that summer.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why do accounts of the workshop’s length differ?</summary>

Nobody kept minutes, people came and went, and historians rely on different notes: from the grant’s five weeks to the eight in Solomonoff’s notes.

</details>

## One summer and today’s timelines

“In one summer” was the first in a long line of promises. Then came forecasts of ten years, of twenty, of “soon.” Today you regularly read that human-level AI is a few years away. Maybe this time the forecast is right. But Dartmouth’s lesson isn’t that the scientists got the dates wrong. It’s about how to read any forecast.

Put four questions to it:
1. **What exactly is promised?** “A significant advance on one problem” can be checked. “Human-level AI” can’t, until someone says how you’d recognize it.
2. **By when?** Without a date, a forecast can’t be proven wrong, so it can’t be judged either.
3. **Who is promising, and what do they gain?** The proposal needed a foundation’s money. Today a forecast can help raise investment or attention. That doesn’t make it false, but it’s worth knowing.
4. **What was promised before?** AI’s history is full of dates that slipped, and of results that sometimes came sooner than anyone expected.

The Dartmouth proposal had honest answers to the first two: a modest goal and one summer. It could be checked, and the check showed that one summer wasn’t enough. That beats a forecast that can never be checked at all.

